WEBVTT

00:00.690 --> 00:03.450
What I want to do in this video
is to prove one of the more

00:03.450 --> 00:08.980
useful results in geometry, and
that's that an inscribed angle

00:08.980 --> 00:14.950
is just an angle who's vertex
sits on the circumference

00:14.950 --> 00:17.080
of the circle.

00:17.080 --> 00:19.800
So that is our inscribed angle.

00:19.800 --> 00:24.950
I'll denote it by si -- I'll
use the si for inscribed angle

00:24.950 --> 00:27.170
and angles in this video.

00:27.170 --> 00:33.530
That si, the inscribed angle,
is going to be exactly 1/2 of

00:33.530 --> 00:37.880
the central angle that
subtends the same arc.

00:37.880 --> 00:40.730
So I just used a lot a fancy
words, but I think you'll

00:40.730 --> 00:41.650
get what I'm saying.

00:41.650 --> 00:42.820
So this is si.

00:42.820 --> 00:44.470
It is an inscribed angle.

00:44.470 --> 00:48.710
It sits, it's vertex sits
on the circumference.

00:48.710 --> 00:52.570
And if you draw out the to rays
that come out from this angle

00:52.570 --> 00:56.040
or the two cords that define
this angle, it intersects the

00:56.040 --> 00:57.340
circle at the other end.

00:57.340 --> 01:00.390
And if you look at the part of
the circumference of the circle

01:00.390 --> 01:03.730
that's inside of it, that
is the arc that is

01:03.730 --> 01:06.160
subtended by si.

01:06.160 --> 01:09.010
It's all very fancy words,
but I think the idea is

01:09.010 --> 01:09.920
pretty straightforward.

01:09.920 --> 01:28.485
This right here is the arc
subtended by si, where si is

01:28.485 --> 01:31.560
that inscribed angle right over
there, the vertex sitting

01:31.560 --> 01:32.400
on the circumference.

01:32.400 --> 01:37.920
Now, a central angle is an
angle where the vertex is

01:37.920 --> 01:39.460
sitting at the center
of the circle.

01:39.460 --> 01:41.880
So let's say that this right
here -- I'll try to eyeball

01:41.880 --> 01:45.510
it -- that right there is
the center of the circle.

01:45.510 --> 01:51.360
So let me draw a central angle
that subtends this same arc.

01:51.360 --> 01:58.470
So that looks like a central
angle subtending that same arc.

01:58.470 --> 01:59.390
Just like that.

01:59.390 --> 02:01.440
Let's call this theta.

02:01.440 --> 02:06.030
So this angle is si, this
angle right here is theta.

02:06.030 --> 02:10.120
When I'm going to prove in this
video is that si is always

02:10.120 --> 02:14.050
going to be equal
to 1/2 of theta.

02:14.050 --> 02:18.220
So if I were to tell you that
si is equal to, I don't know,

02:18.220 --> 02:21.330
25 degrees, then you would
immediately know that theta

02:21.330 --> 02:23.090
must be equal to 50 degrees.

02:23.090 --> 02:26.080
Or if I told you that theta was
80 degrees, then you would

02:26.080 --> 02:29.300
immediately know that
si was 40 degrees.

02:29.300 --> 02:31.500
So let's actually proved this.

02:31.500 --> 02:34.520
So let me clear this.

02:34.520 --> 02:37.730
So a good place to start,
or the place I'm going to

02:37.730 --> 02:40.460
start, is a special case.

02:40.460 --> 02:45.250
I'm going to draw a inscribed
angle, but one of the cords

02:45.250 --> 02:47.910
that define it is going to be
the diameter of the circle.

02:47.910 --> 02:50.526
So this isn't going to be the
general case, this is going

02:50.526 --> 02:51.320
to be a special case.

02:51.320 --> 02:55.325
So let me see, this is the
center right here of my circle.

02:55.325 --> 02:59.030
I'm trying to eyeball it.

02:59.030 --> 03:00.770
Center looks like that.

03:00.770 --> 03:04.210
So let me draw a diameter.

03:04.210 --> 03:06.440
So the diameter
looks like that.

03:06.440 --> 03:09.410
Then let me define
my inscribed angle.

03:09.410 --> 03:11.860
This diameter is
one side of it.

03:11.860 --> 03:15.910
And then the other side
maybe is just like that.

03:15.910 --> 03:20.520
So let me call this
right here si.

03:20.520 --> 03:27.120
If that's si, this length right
here is a radius -- that's

03:27.120 --> 03:29.330
our radius of our circle.

03:29.330 --> 03:33.080
Then this length right here is
also going to be the radius of

03:33.080 --> 03:35.760
our circle going from the
center to the circumference.

03:35.760 --> 03:38.130
Your circumference is defined
by all of the points that are

03:38.130 --> 03:40.340
exactly a radius away
from the center.

03:40.340 --> 03:43.610
So that's also a radius.

03:43.610 --> 03:47.920
Now, this triangle right here
is an isosceles triangle.

03:47.920 --> 03:49.890
It has two sides
that are equal.

03:49.890 --> 03:51.880
Two sides that are
definitely equal.

03:51.880 --> 03:54.630
We know that when we have two
sides being equal, their

03:54.630 --> 03:57.290
base angles are also equal.

03:57.290 --> 04:00.640
So this will also
be equal to si.

04:00.640 --> 04:02.130
You might not recognize
it because it's

04:02.130 --> 04:03.180
tilted up like that.

04:03.180 --> 04:05.720
But I think many of us when we
see a triangle that looks like

04:05.720 --> 04:10.940
this, if I told you this is r
and that is r, that these two

04:10.940 --> 04:17.860
sides are equal, and if this is
si, then you would also

04:17.860 --> 04:20.830
know that this angle is
also going to be si.

04:20.830 --> 04:23.930
Base angles are equivalent
on an isosceles triangle.

04:23.930 --> 04:26.720
So this is si, that is also si.

04:26.720 --> 04:29.770
Now, let me look at
the central angle.

04:29.770 --> 04:32.710
This is the central angle
subtending the same arc.

04:32.710 --> 04:35.920
Let's highlight the arc that
they're both subtending.

04:35.920 --> 04:40.300
This right here is the arc that
they're both going to subtend.

04:40.300 --> 04:44.350
So this is my central
angle right there, theta.

04:44.350 --> 04:49.000
Now if this angle is theta,
what's this angle going to be?

04:49.000 --> 04:50.620
This angle right here.

04:50.620 --> 04:53.010
Well, this angle is
supplementary to theta,

04:53.010 --> 04:56.640
so it's 180 minus theta.

04:56.640 --> 04:59.560
When you add these two angles
together you go 180 degrees

04:59.560 --> 05:01.750
around or the kind
of formal line.

05:01.750 --> 05:03.790
They're supplementary
to each other.

05:03.790 --> 05:06.740
Now we also know that these
three angles are sitting

05:06.740 --> 05:08.260
inside of the same triangle.

05:08.260 --> 05:12.030
So they must add up
to 180 degrees.

05:12.030 --> 05:19.300
So we get si -- this si plus
that si plus si plus this

05:19.300 --> 05:25.420
angle, which is 180 minus
theta plus 180 minus theta.

05:25.420 --> 05:29.130
These three angles must
add up to 180 degrees.

05:29.130 --> 05:31.740
They're the three
angles of a triangle.

05:31.740 --> 05:34.605
Now we could subtract
180 from both sides.

05:37.140 --> 05:43.260
Si plus si is 2 si minus
theta is equal to 0.

05:43.260 --> 05:44.840
Add theta to both sides.

05:44.840 --> 05:48.770
You get 2 si is equal to theta.

05:48.770 --> 05:52.850
Multiply both sides by 1/2
or divide both sides by 2.

05:52.850 --> 05:56.680
You get si is equal
to 1/2 of theta.

05:56.680 --> 06:00.070
So we just proved what we set
out to prove for the special

06:00.070 --> 06:07.120
case where our inscribed angle
is defined, where one on the

06:07.120 --> 06:11.200
rays, if you want to view these
lines as rays, where one of the

06:11.200 --> 06:15.220
rays that defines this
inscribed angle is

06:15.220 --> 06:17.180
along the diameter.

06:17.180 --> 06:19.200
The diameter forms
part of that ray.

06:19.200 --> 06:21.720
So this is a special
case where one edge is

06:21.720 --> 06:23.760
sitting on the diameter.

06:23.760 --> 06:27.660
So already we could
generalize this.

06:27.660 --> 06:30.580
So now that we know that if
this is 50 that this is

06:30.580 --> 06:32.820
going to be 100 degrees
and likewise, right?

06:32.820 --> 06:37.460
Whatever si is or whatever
theta is, si's going to be 1/2

06:37.460 --> 06:40.450
of that, or whatever si is,
theta is going to

06:40.450 --> 06:41.830
be 2 times that.

06:41.830 --> 06:44.110
And now this will
apply for any time.

06:44.110 --> 06:55.440
We could use this notion any
time that -- so just using that

06:55.440 --> 06:59.460
result we just got, we can now
generalize it a little bit,

06:59.460 --> 07:02.890
although this won't apply
to all inscribed angles.

07:02.890 --> 07:05.090
Let's have an inscribed
angle that looks like this.

07:10.680 --> 07:12.980
So this situation, the center,
you can kind of view it as

07:12.980 --> 07:15.470
it's inside of the angle.

07:15.470 --> 07:17.150
That's my inscribed angle.

07:17.150 --> 07:18.890
And I want to find a
relationship between this

07:18.890 --> 07:22.450
inscribed angle and the central
angle that's subtending

07:22.450 --> 07:24.360
to same arc.

07:24.360 --> 07:29.880
So that's my central angle
subtending the same arc.

07:29.880 --> 07:33.550
Well, you might say, hey, gee,
none of these ends or these

07:33.550 --> 07:37.310
cords that define this angle,
neither of these are diameters,

07:37.310 --> 07:40.400
but what we can do is
we can draw a diameter.

07:40.400 --> 07:43.300
If the center is within
these two cords we

07:43.300 --> 07:46.100
can draw a diameter.

07:46.100 --> 07:48.920
We can draw a diameter
just like that.

07:48.920 --> 07:51.680
If we draw a diameter just like
that, if we define this angle

07:51.680 --> 07:55.430
as si 1, that angle as si 2.

07:55.430 --> 07:58.320
Clearly si is the sum
of those two angles.

07:58.320 --> 08:04.350
And we call this angle theta
1, and this angle theta 2.

08:04.350 --> 08:07.240
We immediately you know that,
just using the result I just

08:07.240 --> 08:12.540
got, since we have one side of
our angles in both cases being

08:12.540 --> 08:18.260
a diameter now, we know
that si 1 is going to be

08:18.260 --> 08:22.010
equal to 1/2 theta 1.

08:22.010 --> 08:24.870
And we know that si 2 is
going to be 1/2 theta 2.

08:24.870 --> 08:30.140
Si 2 is going to
be 1/2 theta 2.

08:30.140 --> 08:39.850
So si, which is si 1 plus si 2,
so si 1 plus si 2 is going to

08:39.850 --> 08:41.120
be equal to these two things.

08:41.120 --> 08:47.580
1/2 theta 1 plus 1/2 theta 2.

08:47.580 --> 08:51.180
Si 1 plus si 2, this is equal
to the first inscribed

08:51.180 --> 08:53.850
angle that we want to deal
with, just regular si.

08:53.850 --> 08:54.980
That's si.

08:54.980 --> 08:58.350
And this right here, this
is equal to 1/2 times

08:58.350 --> 09:00.960
theta 1 plus theta 2.

09:00.960 --> 09:03.960
What's theta 1 plus theta 2?

09:03.960 --> 09:06.470
Well that's just our
original theta that

09:06.470 --> 09:08.490
we were dealing with.

09:08.490 --> 09:12.080
So now we see that si
is equal to 1/2 theta.

09:12.080 --> 09:14.710
So now we've proved it for a
slightly more general case

09:14.710 --> 09:20.020
where our center is inside
of the two rays that

09:20.020 --> 09:21.640
define that angle.

09:21.640 --> 09:27.100
Now, we still haven't addressed
a slightly harder situation or

09:27.100 --> 09:33.660
a more general situation where
if this is the center of our

09:33.660 --> 09:39.420
circle and I have an inscribed
angle where the center isn't

09:39.420 --> 09:40.990
sitting inside of
the two cords.

09:40.990 --> 09:41.820
Let me draw that.

09:41.820 --> 09:48.800
So that's going to be my
vertex, and I'll switch colors,

09:48.800 --> 09:51.540
so let's say that is one of the
cords that defines the

09:51.540 --> 09:53.320
angle, just like that.

09:53.320 --> 09:57.860
And let's say that is the
other cord that defines

09:57.860 --> 09:59.170
the angle just like that.

09:59.170 --> 10:02.500
So how do we find the
relationship between, let's

10:02.500 --> 10:07.910
call, this angle right
here, let's call it si 1.

10:07.910 --> 10:13.050
How do we find the relationship
between si 1 and the central

10:13.050 --> 10:16.160
angle that subtends
this same arc?

10:16.160 --> 10:19.530
So when I talk about the same
arc, that's that right there.

10:19.530 --> 10:22.720
So the central angle that
subtends the same arc

10:22.720 --> 10:23.660
will look like this.

10:28.150 --> 10:32.910
Let's call that theta 1.

10:32.910 --> 10:36.770
What we can do is use what we
just learned when one side of

10:36.770 --> 10:39.350
our inscribed angle
is a diameter.

10:39.350 --> 10:41.135
So let's construct that.

10:41.135 --> 10:44.260
So let me draw a diameter here.

10:44.260 --> 10:47.010
The result we want still is
that this should be 1/2 of

10:47.010 --> 10:48.180
this, but let's prove it.

10:48.180 --> 10:57.560
Let's draw a diameter
just like that.

10:57.560 --> 11:09.490
Let me call this angle right
here, let me call that si 2.

11:09.490 --> 11:14.770
And it is subtending this arc
right there -- let me do

11:14.770 --> 11:16.140
that in a darker color.

11:16.140 --> 11:19.770
It is subtending this
arc right there.

11:19.770 --> 11:22.360
So the central angle that
subtends that same arc,

11:22.360 --> 11:25.300
let me call that theta 2.

11:25.300 --> 11:30.890
Now, we know from the earlier
part of this video that si

11:30.890 --> 11:37.600
2 is going to be equal
to 1/2 theta 2, right?

11:37.600 --> 11:40.760
They share -- the
diameter is right there.

11:40.760 --> 11:44.300
The diameter is one of the
cords that forms the angle.

11:44.300 --> 11:47.500
So si 2 is going to be
equal to 1/2 theta 2.

11:50.140 --> 11:52.810
This is exactly what we've been
doing in the last video, right?

11:52.810 --> 11:55.430
This is an inscribed angle.

11:55.430 --> 11:59.550
One of the cords that define
is sitting on the diameter.

11:59.550 --> 12:02.740
So this is going to be 1/2 of
this angle, of the central

12:02.740 --> 12:05.980
angle that subtends
the same arc.

12:05.980 --> 12:09.000
Now, let's look at
this larger angle.

12:09.000 --> 12:11.680
This larger angle right here.

12:11.680 --> 12:14.240
Si 1 plus si 2.

12:14.240 --> 12:22.720
Right, that larger angle
is si 1 plus si 2.

12:22.720 --> 12:28.680
Once again, this subtends this
entire arc right here, and it

12:28.680 --> 12:32.100
has a diameter as one of the
cords that defines

12:32.100 --> 12:34.310
this huge angle.

12:34.310 --> 12:37.380
So this is going to be 1/2
of the central angle that

12:37.380 --> 12:38.580
subtends the same arc.

12:38.580 --> 12:42.270
We're just using what we've
already shown in this video.

12:42.270 --> 12:47.390
So this is going to be equal to
1/2 of this huge central angle

12:47.390 --> 12:51.370
of theta 1 plus theta 2.

12:54.310 --> 12:56.530
So far we've just used
everything that we've learned

12:56.530 --> 12:58.160
earlier in this video.

12:58.160 --> 13:03.160
Now, we already know that si
2 is equal to 1/2 theta 2.

13:03.160 --> 13:05.630
So let me make that
substitution.

13:05.630 --> 13:07.030
This is equal to that.

13:07.030 --> 13:15.330
So we can say that si 1 plus
-- instead of si 2 I'll write

13:15.330 --> 13:26.630
1/2 theta 2 is equal to 1/2
theta 1 plus 1/2 theta 2.

13:30.340 --> 13:34.020
We can subtract 1/2 theta
2 from both sides, and

13:34.020 --> 13:35.740
we get our result.

13:35.740 --> 13:40.900
Si 1 is equal to 1/2 theta one.

13:40.900 --> 13:41.970
And now we're done.

13:41.970 --> 13:44.990
We have proven the situation
that the inscribed angle is

13:44.990 --> 13:50.680
always 1/2 of the central angle
that subtends the same arc,

13:50.680 --> 13:53.980
regardless of whether the
center of the circle is inside

13:53.980 --> 13:58.990
of the angle, outside of the
angle, whether we have a

13:58.990 --> 14:00.950
diameter on one side.

14:00.950 --> 14:05.860
So any other angle can be
constructed as a sum of

14:05.860 --> 14:08.300
any or all of these that
we've already done.

14:08.300 --> 14:10.190
So hopefully you found this
useful and now we can actually

14:10.190 --> 14:14.630
build on this result to do some
more interesting

14:14.630 --> 14:16.460
geometry proofs.
